  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"rschema_0509\" comprises seven tables: Patient, Department, Doctor, Ward, Hospitalization_Record, Registration_Record, and Nurse.\nThe Patient table contains information about patients, including their age, emergency contact phone number, gender, identity card number, medical record number, name, patient ID, and phone number. The Department table represents departments within the hospital, with columns for contact phone number, department head ID, department ID, department name, and location. The Doctor table holds information about doctors, including contact phone number, department ID, doctor ID, gender, name, and title. The Ward table contains information about wards in the hospital, including bed count, head ID, location, remaining bed count, and ward ID. The Hospitalization_Record table records hospitalization details, including attending physician ID, bed number, discharge time, hospitalization start time, patient ID, and ward ID. The Registration_Record table holds consultation records, including consultation time, diagnosis result, doctor ID, and patient ID. Finally, the Nurse table contains information about nurses, including department ID, duties, gender, name, nurse ID, phone number, and title. All tables have primary keys and foreign keys to ensure data integrity and referential integrity.",
  "gold_question": "The database is an inpatient information management system used to record inpatient information, medical processes, and manage related resources (such as wards, departments, doctors, etc.). The system assists hospitals in efficiently managing patient hospitalization status, doctors' treatment processes, ward resource usage, and other information to ensure smooth medical operations. The system primarily includes the following tables:\n\n1. Patient  \nFields: Patient ID, Name, Gender, Age, Hospitalization Time, Ward, Phone, Identity Card Number, Medical Record Number, Emergency Contact Phone  \nBusiness Requirement: Record basic patient information, including patient name, contact information, hospitalization time, and ward, etc. Through this table, hospitals can manage and query basic patient details to facilitate subsequent treatment, medical advice, and tracking of medical records.\n\n2. Department  \nFields: Department ID, Department Name, Department Head ID, Contact Phone, Location  \nBusiness Requirement: Record information of each hospital department, including department name, head, and department location, etc. Through this table, hospitals can manage departments and allocate resources such as doctors and nurses.\n\n3. Doctor  \nFields: Doctor ID, Name, Gender, Contact Phone, Title, Department ID  \nBusiness Requirement: Record doctor information, including name, title, contact information, and department affiliation. The doctor table is associated with the department table to help hospitals manage doctor resources and arrange patient consultations.\n\n4. Ward  \nFields: Ward ID, Location, Head ID, Bed Count, Remaining Bed Count  \nBusiness Requirement: Record basic ward information, including ward location, head (doctor), bed count, and remaining bed count. Through this table, hospitals can efficiently manage ward resources, allocate beds reasonably, and ensure patients can be accommodated promptly.\n\n5. Nurse  \nFields: Nurse ID, Name, Gender, Phone, Department ID, Duties, Title  \nBusiness Requirement: Record basic nurse information, including name, contact information, title, duties, and department affiliation. The nurse table is associated with the department table for managing nurse allocation, duties, and patient care relationships.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_deepseek/0509.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0509\nCREATE TABLE `patient` (\n  `patient_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `age` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) NOT NULL,\n  `identity_card_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `medical_record_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_number` varchar(15) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `emergency_contact_phone` varchar(15) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`patient_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `department` (\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `department_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_phone` varchar(15) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `department_head_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`department_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `doctor` (\n  `doctor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_phone` varchar(15) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`doctor_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `doctor_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `department` (`department_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `ward` (\n  `ward_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `bed_count` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `remaining_bed_count` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `head_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`ward_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `hospitalization_record` (\n  `record_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `patient_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ward_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `attending_physician_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `bed_number` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `hospitalization_start_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `discharge_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`record_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `hospitalization_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`patient_id`) REFERENCES `patient` (`patient_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `hospitalization_record_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`ward_id`) REFERENCES `ward` (`ward_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `hospitalization_record_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`attending_physician_id`) REFERENCES `doctor` (`doctor_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `registration_record` (\n  `record_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `patient_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `doctor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `consultation_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `diagnosis_result` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`record_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `registration_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`patient_id`) REFERENCES `patient` (`patient_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `registration_record_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`doctor_id`) REFERENCES `doctor` (`doctor_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `nurse` (\n  `nurse_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_number` varchar(15) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`nurse_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `nurse_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `department` (`department_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
